WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
[Archival voiceover]: It was the same Soviet&nbsp;
story of shrouding their space research&nbsp;in secrecy.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.065
[Music]&nbsp;

00:00:05.065 --> 00:00:11.600
[Narrator]: In 1966, the Soviet Union’s&nbsp;
Luna 9 probe, cushioned by airbags,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.590
successfully landed on the Moon

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.080
and returned&nbsp;the first pictures from the surface.

00:00:16.827 --> 00:00:19.544
Ever since, governments have dominated

00:00:19.544 --> 00:00:22.640
the design and delivery of lunar landers,

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:28.640
with the United States, China,&nbsp;Israel, and India all making attempts.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.400
Now, the action is shifting from governments to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.392
a number of commercial outfits that

00:00:34.392 --> 00:00:36.800
are trying to make lunar exploration happen

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.223
on fast schedules and lean budgets.

00:00:40.223 --> 00:00:44.706
Next year, one company’s lander is set to touch down on the Moon

00:00:44.706 --> 00:00:47.920
where it will&nbsp;hunt for a critical and scarce resource…

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:49.460
Water.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.840
Astrobotic, a startup based in an&nbsp;unassuming Pittsburgh warehouse,&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.527
has received a slice of NASA’s 2.6-billion-dollar&nbsp;program

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:03.976
intended to galvanize the private sector’s development

00:01:03.976 --> 00:01:06.676
of lunar landers and rovers.

00:01:06.676 --> 00:01:09.780
Commercial Lunar Payload Services, or CLPS [pron. "clips"],

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.062
is supporting Astrobotic and similar companies

00:01:13.062 --> 00:01:16.302
with the goal of sending landers to the Moon twice a year—

00:01:16.302 --> 00:01:19.120
along with a&nbsp;bevy of scientific instruments.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.640
First up for Astrobotic is&nbsp;Peregrine, a treehouse-sized lander

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.807
expected to launch from Cape Canaveral,&nbsp;Florida,

00:01:27.807 --> 00:01:29.853
next year aboard a Vulcan rocket.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.077
It will chart a course for Lacus Mortis,

00:01:33.077 --> 00:01:36.151
a&nbsp;smooth plain of lava near the Moon’s equator.

00:01:36.151 --> 00:01:40.430
The capabilities of the 21 instrument payloads&nbsp;aboard Peregrine

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:44.620
will be somewhat limited, since the lander will be stuck in one spot.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.702
But it will provide a baseline

00:01:46.702 --> 00:01:49.520
for how landers&nbsp;contaminate the surface with their own gases,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.162
which confounds the hunt for water,

00:01:52.162 --> 00:01:55.120
as well as show&nbsp;how its payload of instruments work together.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.343
In 2023, Astrobotic plans&nbsp;to launch a larger lander,&nbsp;Griffin, 

00:02:01.343 --> 00:02:04.960
which will ferry the&nbsp;NASA-engineered VIPER rover to the Moon.&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.872
Its instruments should determine not only

00:02:08.872 --> 00:02:12.335
the&nbsp;trace abundances of water, but also its origin:

00:02:12.335 --> 00:02:17.680
How much of it comes from solar wind&nbsp;
versus asteroids or comet collisions?

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.464
Astrobotic isn't the only private company

00:02:21.464 --> 00:02:25.136
working under the auspices of NASA’s CLPS program.

00:02:25.136 --> 00:02:29.053
Houston-based Intuitive Machines will launch&nbsp;its Nova-C

00:02:29.053 --> 00:02:31.461
around the same time as Peregrine,

00:02:31.461 --> 00:02:35.939
and another lander—built by Masten Space Systems&nbsp;in California—

00:02:35.939 --> 00:02:38.160
is set to launch in 2023.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.943
If all goes well, the lander program will deliver

00:02:41.943 --> 00:02:46.684
a steady supply of experiments across wide-ranging fields of science.

00:02:46.684 --> 00:02:50.051
Researchers are planning&nbsp;an instrument to probe the Moon’s interior

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.636
and another to snap the first-ever picture of

00:02:53.636 --> 00:02:56.160
Earth’s magnetic field from outside the planet.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.136
Even astronomers will hitch a ride,

00:02:59.136 --> 00:03:02.720
as a radio&nbsp;telescope is placed on the Moon’s far side,

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.804
where it can scan the cosmos amid a quiet&nbsp;unknown on Earth.

00:03:07.804 --> 00:03:12.080
[Music]

